{"tool_calls": [{"id": "toolu_bdrk_016FgM5Fh8vX4AzwU7KGZk5k", "name": "file_read", "arguments": {"file_path": "summary/carbon_aware_routing_protocol_summary.json"}}]}成功执行，结果为tool_result:{"status": "success", "output": "{
  "content": "{\n  \"title\": \"A carbon-aware routing protocol for optimizing carbon emissions in modular construction logistics\",\n  \"sections\": {\n    \"abstract\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The paper proposes a Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) network framework for modular construction logistics (MCL) to integrate multiple actors and reduce carbon emissions. It introduces a carbon-aware routing protocol that considers module weight, travel distance, and carbon emission factors to optimize routing decisions with the lowest carbon emissions. Numerical studies demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed routing algorithm in reducing environmental impact, contributing to sustainability goals in the construction industry.\"\n    },\n    \"introduction\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The introduction highlights modular construction as a sustainable approach in the construction industry that can mitigate environmental impact and enhance building efficiency. It emphasizes the importance of managing carbon emissions in module construction logistics to achieve carbon neutrality. The paper identifies two key problems: (1) how to integrate different actors to achieve resource sharing in MCL, and (2) how to improve logistics management with carbon reduction in MCL. These problems are addressed through a CPI network framework that provides an integrated solution and optimizes carbon reduction in routing protocols.\"\n    },\n    \"cpi_network_framework\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The CPI network framework establishes a layered architecture based on Physical Internet (PI) characteristics, with a cyber layer for integration and information collaboration. The framework includes key elements such as CPI routers at suppliers, borders, and manufacturers to connect MCL, and CPI routing tables that display routing decisions based on metrics like lowest carbon emissions. The framework introduces three area networks: CPI-Local Area Network (LAN) for internal logistics management, CPI-Catchment Area Network (CAN) for integrating similar enterprises in a city, and CPI-Wide Area Network (WAN) for connecting different CPI-CANs across a large geographical area.\"\n    },\n    \"operations_mechanism\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The operations mechanism in the CPI network helps MCL provide an integrated solution for multiple actors across different responsibilities and cities. In CPI-LAN, enterprises manage internal logistics with routers at key points like warehouses and yards. In CPI-CAN, multiple enterprises providing common services in a city are connected, allowing manufacturers to place orders through gateways based on demand and traffic information. The CPI-WAN connects different CPI-CANs across large geographical areas, with border routers updating information between networks for subsequent transport.\"\n    },\n    \"routing_protocols_design\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The paper introduces a carbon emissions routing protocol that integrates a carbon emissions algorithm from life cycle assessment with the A* algorithm from the Internet. The routing protocol changes the metric for making decisions from shortest path to lowest carbon emissions. The routing table displays carbon emissions for each road, with the first router showing total carbon emission for the shipment and subsequent routers showing emissions from current node to final node. The system provides routing suggestions based on current traffic information and link availability.\"\n    },\n    \"numerical_studies\": {\n      \"summary\": \"To verify the routing protocol, the paper conducts scenario analysis in the Greater Bay Area with suppliers and manufacturers at different locations. The study uses medium and heavy trucks with different load capacities (8t, 10t, and 30t), assumes fully loaded trucks with different carbon emission factors, and considers shipments from one supplier to one manufacturer. The results show that the integrated solution under the CPI network achieves the lowest carbon emissions for shipments compared to other scenarios.\"\n    },\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The paper concludes with three major innovations: (1) establishing an integrated solution using LAN, WAN, and CAN concepts in the CPI network, (2) introducing a carbon-aware routing protocol to help reduce emissions during transportation, and (3) providing a managerial implementation that extends beyond MCL to the broader logistics sector, offering a foundation for more informed routing decisions that can reduce empty transports and unnecessary repetitive transportation.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"key_contributions\": [\n    \"Development of a Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) network framework that integrates multiple actors in modular construction logistics\",\n    \"Introduction of a carbon-aware routing protocol that optimizes carbon emissions in transportation\",\n    \"Establishment of area networks (CPI-LAN, CPI-CAN, CPI-WAN) for effective resource sharing and logistics management\",\n    \"Demonstration through numerical studies that the integrated CPI solution achieves the lowest carbon emissions compared to traditional approaches\"\n  ],\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"authors\": [\"Chung-lam Ng\", \"Hang Wu\", \"Ming Li\", \"Ray Y. Zhong\", \"Xinye Qu\", \"George Q. Huang\"],\n    \"publication\": \"2024 IEEE 20th International Conference on Automation Science and Engineering (CASE)\",\n    \"year\": \"2024\",\n    \"source_file\": \"upload/A carbon-aware routing protocol for optimizing carbon emissions in modular construction logistics.pdf\"\n  }\n}",
  "total_lines": 38,
  "line_range": "1-38",
  "file_path": "summary/carbon_aware_routing_protocol_summary.json",
  "container_path": "/workspace/tasks/agent_test/summary/carbon_aware_routing_protocol_summary.json"
