  "content": "{\n  \"title\": \"The research landscape around the physical internet – a bibliometric analysis\",\n  \"authors\": [\"Christopher Münch\", \"Manuel Wehrle\", \"Tobias Kuhn\", \"Evi Hartmann\"],\n  \"published_in\": \"International Journal of Production Research\",\n  \"publication_year\": \"2024\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1080/00207543.2023.2205969\",\n  \"sections\": {\n    \"abstract\": {\n      \"summary\": \"This paper provides a bibliometric analysis of the Physical Internet (PI) research landscape. Unlike existing qualitative literature reviews, it uses quantitative methods (bibliometric performance analysis and science mapping) to provide a holistic overview of PI concepts, dimensions, and interrelations. The study identifies key journals, researchers, and institutions in the PI field, and categorizes research into eight thematic clusters. Based on this analysis, the paper suggests directions for future research.\"\n    },\n    \"introduction\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The introduction explains how supply chains have evolved into complex global networks spanning multiple countries and continents. Despite improvements in supply chain management, existing approaches are reaching their limits in terms of economic, environmental, and social sustainability. The Physical Internet (PI) is presented as a paradigm-breaking concept that could address these challenges. Inspired by the Digital Internet, the PI aims to create a logistics system characterized by standardized containers, collaborative routing, and interconnected networks for efficient product movement. The section traces the development of PI from its first mention in 2006 to its formal conceptualization by Professor Benoit Montreuil and subsequent research initiatives. It also highlights significant interest in PI from industry, policy makers, and academia, noting the establishment of research centers and special journal issues dedicated to the topic.\"\n    },\n    \"methodology\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The methodology section explains the bibliometric analysis approach used, which combines performance analysis (examining research performance of authors and institutions) and science mapping (identifying structural relationships among research works). The data collection process involved: (1) defining search terms ('Physical Internet') and database (Scopus), (2) screening initial results, (3) refining collected data, and (4) analyzing initial data statistics. The search covered publications from February 12, 2011, to May 31, 2021, initially yielding 246 documents, which after refinement resulted in a final dataset of 177 articles. These were analyzed using Bibexcel software for bibliometric performance analysis and Gephi for science mapping through bibliographic coupling.\"\n    },\n    \"findings_from_bibliometric_performance_analysis\": {\n      \"summary\": \"This section presents findings on how PI literature has evolved, focusing on affiliation statistics, author influence, and keyword statistics. The analysis reveals increasing research interest in PI over the past decade, with significant growth in publications during 2019-2020. The top 10 journals account for about 50% of published articles, with the International Journal of Production Research leading (22 articles). Institutionally, European organizations (particularly from France) are the largest contributors, with Mines ParisTech and Université Polytechnique Hauts-de-France being most prominent, followed by North American institutions like Georgia Institute of Technology and Université Laval. The analysis identifies Eric Ballot (Mines ParisTech) and Benoit Montreuil (Georgia Institute of Technology) as the most contributing authors (23 publications each), closely followed by Shenle Pan (20 publications). These pioneers have frequently collaborated on PI research, focusing mainly on mathematical modeling and simulation approaches.\"\n    },\n    \"findings_from_bibliometric_science_mapping\": {\n      \"summary\": \"This section identifies eight distinct research communities within PI literature through bibliographic coupling analysis. The communities represent different thematic focuses: (1) PI benefits and operationalization challenges, (2) carriers and shippers in PI networks, (3) PI foundations, (4) smart product-service systems and services, (5) hyperconnected city logistics, (6) information and communication technology requirements, (7) modular PI containers, and (8) organization and control of PI hubs. For each community, the section details key papers and their contributions to understanding different aspects of the PI concept.\"\n    },\n    \"discussion_of_findings\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The discussion compares findings with existing qualitative literature reviews by Sternberg and Norrman (2017) and Treiblmaier et al. (2020). While there are similarities in identified themes, this quantitative analysis provides more detailed insights. The authors identify four promising directions for future PI research: (1) practical demonstration of PI in real-world contexts beyond theoretical concepts, including implementation for large-scale global structures and standardization considerations; (2) addressing 'softer factors' like trust, performance monitoring, pricing mechanisms, and power relations; (3) clarifying legal frameworks for divided logistical services in PI; and (4) exploring how PI can leverage digitalization through data sharing, privacy, security, and autonomous systems. A fifth direction suggests investigating how PI can be integrated with emerging supply chain concepts like intertwined supply networks, reconfigurable supply chains, and supply ecosystems.\"\n    },\n    \"conclusion\": {\n      \"summary\": \"The conclusion summarizes the study's contributions to understanding PI research developments. It confirms growing interest in PI over the past decade, with diversity across eight different focus areas. These include PI benefits and implementation challenges, physical and IT requirements (containers and ICT), smart product-service systems, network elements (carriers, shippers, hubs), hyperconnected city logistics, and foundational PI concepts. The authors acknowledge limitations of using only the Scopus database and excluding sources like book chapters and reviews, suggesting future bibliometric analyses could incorporate multiple databases for a more comprehensive picture.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"research_communities\": {\n    \"community_1\": {\n      \"name\": \"PI benefits and operationalization challenges\",\n      \"key_papers\": [\"Dong and Franklin (2021)\", \"Meyer, Kuhn, and Hartmann (2019)\", \"Plasch et al. (2021)\", \"Sternberg and Denizel (2021)\"],\n      \"focus\": \"Benefits of PI compared to conventional logistics systems and challenges in implementing the PI concept\"\n    },\n    \"community_2\": {\n      \"name\": \"Carriers and shippers in the PI network\",\n      \"key_papers\": [\"Qiao, Pan, and Ballot (2019)\", \"Pan, Chen, and Zhong (2015)\", \"Ji, Guan, and Liu (2021)\"],\n      \"focus\": \"Roles and perspectives of carriers and shippers in the PI, including dynamic pricing, utilizing additional loading capacities, and container selection\"\n    },\n    \"community_3\": {\n      \"name\": \"PI foundations\",\n      \"key_papers\": [\"Montreuil, Meller, and Ballot (2012a)\", \"Sarraj et al. (2014a)\", \"Osmólski, Voronina, and Koliński (2019)\"],\n      \"focus\": \"Fundamental concepts, components, and principles of PI, especially suitable for newcomers to the field\"\n    },\n    \"community_4\": {\n      \"name\": \"Smart product-service systems and services\",\n      \"key_papers\": [\"Pan (2019)\", \"Zhong et al. (2017)\"],\n      \"focus\": \"Innovative product-service systems required by or enabled through PI implementation\"\n    },\n    \"community_5\": {\n      \"name\": \"Hyperconnected city logistics\",\n      \"key_papers\": [\"Crainic and Montreuil (2016)\", \"Crainic, Gendreau, and Jemai (2020)\", \"Kim et al. (2021)\"],\n      \"focus\": \"Integration of city logistics with PI concepts, creating hyperconnected urban logistics systems\"\n    },\n    \"community_6\": {\n      \"name\": \"ICT requirements\",\n      \"key_papers\": [\"Tran-Dang and Kim (2018)\", \"Sallez et al. (2016)\"],\n      \"focus\": \"Information and communication technology requirements for tracking, moving, and storing PI containers\"\n    },\n    \"community_7\": {\n      \"name\": \"Modular PI containers\",\n      \"key_papers\": [\"Landschützer, Ehrentraut, and Jodin (2015)\", \"Lin et al. (2014)\", \"Meller, Lin, and Ellis (2012)\"],\n      \"focus\": \"Development and optimization of modular containers as key enablers for PI realization\"\n    },\n    \"community_8\": {\n      \"name\": \"Organization and control of PI hubs\",\n      \"key_papers\": [\"Maslarić, Nikoličić, and Mirčetić (2016)\", \"Fergani et al. (2019)\", \"Chargui et al. (2020)\"],\n      \"focus\": \"Organization and control of the PI network with emphasis on PI hubs and their efficient operation\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"future_research_directions\": {\n    \"direction_1\": {\n      \"theme\": \"Practical demonstration and large-scale implementation\",\n      \"description\": \"Move from theoretical concepts to practical demonstration in real-world contexts, especially large-scale global implementations. Address standardization of containers and handling processes across different transportation modes.\"\n    },\n    \"direction_2\": {\n      \"theme\": \"Soft factors in PI implementation\",\n      \"description\": \"Explore trust, performance monitoring mechanisms, pricing mechanisms, and power relations within PI networks.\"\n    },\n    \"direction_3\": {\n      \"theme\": \"Legal frameworks\",\n      \"description\": \"Clarify contractual obligations and regulations needed to enable the division of logistical services among various actors in PI.\"\n    },\n    \"direction_4\": {\n      \"theme\": \"PI and digitalization\",\n      \"description\": \"Research data sharing standards, privacy, security, industrial communication of assets, and smart contracts to enable autonomous and self-organizing systems.\"\n    },\n    \"direction_5\": {\n      \"theme\": \"Integration with emerging supply chain concepts\",\n      \"description\": \"Explore how PI can be integrated with and enable new supply chain concepts like intertwined supply networks, reconfigurable supply chains, and supply ecosystems.\"\n    }\n  }\n}",
